<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chapel of the Holy Shroud</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1668">1668</a>–<a href="/years/1694">1694</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Guarini Chapel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chapel of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shroud-of-turin">Holy Shroud</a> is a baroque chapel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, between the cathedral and the royal palace, built to house the Shroud of Turin. Designed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guarino-guarini">Guarino Guarini</a> from 1668 and completed in 1694, it is crowned by a dome of stacked arches rising in a cone. Badly damaged by fire in 1997, it reopened after restoration in 2018.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chapel-of-the-holy-shroud.jpg" alt="Chapel of the Holy Shroud" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Trappy · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chapel_of_Holy_Shroud_Cupola.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
